From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: sun60i-a733: add the A733 PCIe root complex

[Recipe cut: the stop_after_link module parameter is not carried.]

The controller is a DesignWare core: config space at the DBI base, the unrolled
iATU at DBI + 0x300000 with 0x200 per region, 16 regions each way, the DWC MSI
receiver on its own SPI. drivers/pci/controller/dwc/ implements all of that, so
this driver is only what is Allwinner's -- clocks, resets, the combo-PHY, the
board's PERST line, and two registers:

  UDS + 0x0c00  PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE  bit0 starts link training, [7:4] device type
  UDS + 0x0e0c  SII_INT_1          bit0 SMLH link up, bit1 RDLH link up

Both are tabulated in the A733 UM V1.00, PCIe chapter, which documents the whole
User Defined Space. The DBI base is NOT where the UM prints the module base: that
address, 0x06400000, is the UDS, and the DBI sits 0x400000 below it at
0x06000000. Measured at the U-Boot prompt on the 6 GiB board -- 0x06000000 reads 0xabcd1f6d,
the root complex's own vendor/device id.

NOT A PORT OF THE VENDOR DRIVER, ON PURPOSE

The vendor BSP ships a PCIe driver for this controller that does not use the
DesignWare core at all: 3200 lines reimplementing the iATU programming, the MSI
controller and the config accessors. Using the shared core instead means the
A733 gets the behaviour every other DWC platform is tested with -- including the
parts the vendor's copy leaves out, per-vector MSI masking among them, which
their own tree needed a separate patch for. The aim is to cover more than a
port would, and here that is done by deleting the port rather than writing it.

WHAT THE DRIVER DOES BEYOND THE CORE

 - Brings the three resets up in the order the UM's own sequence uses: power-up,
   then controller, then the ITS in front of it, so the controller never leaves
   reset onto an unclocked bus.
 - Holds the supply the board needs. On the A7S there is no 3.3 V slot rail:
   the FPC connector feeds an adapter with its own regulator, whose enable is a
   GPIO, and it is modelled as vpcie3v3-supply.
 - Releases PERST# last, once the PHY's reference clock is live, and then waits
   the 100 ms the CEM spec asks for before anything queries the device. Skipping
   that wait is how a healthy drive enumerates on some boots and not others.
 - Forces the device-type field to root complex in start_link(). It resets to
   RC, so this is a no-op cold -- but a warm start finds whatever the boot loader
   left, and an endpoint-shaped root complex fails in ways that look like a dead
   link.
 - Treats link-up as SMLH AND RDLH, not either: a link with only the physical
   layer up has trained but cannot carry a TLP.

TWO THINGS THIS COST, BOTH MEASURED ON THE BOARD

1. `pp.ops` must be set even when there is nothing to put in it.
   dw_pcie_host_init() dereferences it without a NULL check, so leaving it out
   is an oops during probe rather than a default. The struct here is empty and
   says why.

2. **The AXI slave clock has to be 400 MHz.** Its mux resets to PERI0_600M, and
   at that rate the controller does not survive a configuration read into the
   slave window: the access never completes and the CPU that issued it hangs
   with interrupts off. Measured 2026-09-21 -- a hard lockup on CPU 6 during the
   first scan of bus 01, 20 s later the watchdog's own detector called it, panic,
   reboot. Every gate was open and the link was trained at Gen3 x1 at the time;
   the clock was the only thing wrong. With clk_set_rate(400 MHz) in probe the
   register reads 0x82000000 -- the same value the vendor stack leaves behind --
   and the scan completes in milliseconds.

   The vendor BSP sets that rate from its own clock setup and flags this
   controller generation `pcie_slv_clk_400m`; the UM lists 010 = PERI0_400M as a
   source of the mux without saying it is required. So the number is theirs and
   the failure it prevents is ours.

HARDWARE PROOF (the 6 GiB board, 2026-09-21, kernel 6.18.36-pcie1)

    sun60i-a733-pcie 6000000.pcie: iATU: unroll T, 8 ob, 8 ib, align 4K
    sun60i-a733-pcie 6000000.pcie: PCIe Gen.3 x1 link up
    pci 0000:01:00.0: [1c5c:1327] type 00 class 0x010802 PCIe Endpoint
    nvme nvme0: pci function 0000:01:00.0
    nvme nvme0: 8/0/0 default/read/poll queues
    nvme0n1: p1 p2

The drive is a BC501 NVMe SK hynix 256GB. Silicon after the fact: link status
0x30130C42 (Gen3, x1, DLL active), LTSSM 0x11 = L0, the controller's own link
register 0x02000013 (SMLH + RDLH). 256 MiB read with O_DIRECT at 544 MB/s.

⚠ Worth stating because it is the point of not porting the vendor driver: the
interrupts come up as DW-PCI-MSIX, one vector per queue (nvme0q0..q7). The
vendor's tree needed a separate patch for per-vector masking. None of that is
here; the shared DesignWare code does it.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
@@ -256,6 +256,17 @@ config PCIE_TEGRA194_EP
 	  in order to enable device-specific features PCIE_TEGRA194_EP must be
 	  selected. This uses the DesignWare core.
 
+config PCIE_SUN60I_A733
+	bool "Allwinner A733 PCIe controller"
+	depends on ARCH_SUNXI || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on PCI_MSI
+	select PCIE_DW_HOST
+	help
+	  Say Y here to enable PCIe controller support on Allwinner A733
+	  (sun60iw2) SoCs. The controller is a DesignWare core; this driver
+	  adds the SoC's clocks, resets, combo-PHY and PERST handling on top
+	  of the shared DesignWare host code.
+
 config PCIE_DW_PLAT
 	bool
 
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Makefile b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Makefile
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Makefile
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_DW_DEBUGFS) += pcie-designware-debugfs.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_DW_HOST) += pcie-designware-host.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_DW_EP) += pcie-designware-ep.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_DW_PLAT) += pcie-designware-plat.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_SUN60I_A733) += pcie-sun60i-a733.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_AMD_MDB) += pcie-amd-mdb.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PCIE_BT1) += pcie-bt1.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PCI_DRA7XX) += pci-dra7xx.o
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
@@ -0,0 +1,356 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * PCIe root complex for the Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2).
+ *
+ * The controller is a DesignWare core: config space at the DBI base, the
+ * unrolled iATU at DBI + 0x300000 with 0x200 per region, 16 inbound and 16
+ * outbound regions, the DWC MSI receiver on its own interrupt. All of that is
+ * what drivers/pci/controller/dwc/ already implements, so this file is only
+ * the parts that are Allwinner's: the clocks, the resets, the PHY, the board's
+ * PERST line, and two registers in a window the UM calls the User Defined
+ * Space.
+ *
+ *   UDS + 0x0c00  PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE   bit0 starts link training,
+ *                                     [7:4] device type, reset value 4 = RC
+ *   UDS + 0x0e0c  SII_INT_1           bit0 SMLH link up, bit1 RDLH link up
+ *
+ * Both documented in the A733 UM V1.00, PCIe chapter 18.3, which tabulates the
+ * whole User Defined Space. The DBI base itself is NOT where the UM prints it:
+ * the module base in the memory map is the UDS at 0x06400000, and the DBI sits
+ * 0x400000 below at 0x06000000 -- measured on hardware,
+ * the 6 GiB board at the U-Boot prompt.
+ *
+ * ⚠ The vendor BSP ships a PCIe driver for this controller that does not use
+ * the DesignWare core at all: it reimplements the iATU programming, the MSI
+ * controller and the config-space accessors in about 3200 lines. This driver
+ * deliberately does not port that. Using the shared core means the A733 gets
+ * the iATU, MSI and ECAM behaviour that every other DWC platform is tested
+ * with, including the parts the vendor's copy leaves out -- per-vector MSI
+ * masking among them, which the vendor's own tree needed a patch for.
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 DockSeed
+ */
+
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
+#include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/phy/phy.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
+
+#include "pcie-designware.h"
+
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_CTRL		0x0c00
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE		BIT(0)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_DEVICE_TYPE		GENMASK(7, 4)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_DEVICE_TYPE_RC	0x4
+
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_STAT		0x0e0c
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SMLH_LINK_UP		BIT(0)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_RDLH_LINK_UP		BIT(1)
+
+/*
+ * PCIe CEM 3.0: at least 100 ms between the reference clock being valid and
+ * PERST# being released, and the spec's T_RRS before the first config request.
+ * dw_pcie_wait_for_link() covers the second; this covers the first.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_T_PVPERL_MS		100
+
+enum {
+	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AUX,
+	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AXI_SLV,
+	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_ITS,
+	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_SLV_ACLK,
+	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_MST_ACLK,
+	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_HCLK,
+};
+
+static const char * const sun60i_a733_pcie_clks[] = {
+	[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AUX]	= "aux",
+	[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AXI_SLV]	= "axi-slv",
+	[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_ITS]	= "its",
+	[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_SLV_ACLK]	= "slv-aclk",
+	[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_MST_ACLK]	= "mst-aclk",
+	[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_HCLK]	= "hclk",
+};
+
+struct sun60i_a733_pcie {
+	struct dw_pcie		pci;
+	void __iomem		*app;
+	struct clk_bulk_data	clks[ARRAY_SIZE(sun60i_a733_pcie_clks)];
+	struct reset_control	*rst_pwrup;
+	struct reset_control	*rst_core;
+	struct reset_control	*rst_its;
+	struct phy		*phy;
+	struct gpio_desc	*perst;
+	struct regulator	*vpcie3v3;
+};
+
+static struct sun60i_a733_pcie *to_sun60i_a733_pcie(struct dw_pcie *pci)
+{
+	return container_of(pci, struct sun60i_a733_pcie, pci);
+}
+
+static int sun60i_a733_pcie_start_link(struct dw_pcie *pci)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = to_sun60i_a733_pcie(pci);
+	u32 val;
+
+	val = readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_CTRL);
+	/*
+	 * The device-type field resets to root complex, so this is a no-op on a
+	 * cold start -- but a warm one can find whatever the boot loader left,
+	 * and an endpoint-shaped root complex fails in ways that look like a
+	 * dead link.
+	 */
+	val &= ~SUN60I_A733_PCIE_DEVICE_TYPE;
+	val |= FIELD_PREP(SUN60I_A733_PCIE_DEVICE_TYPE,
+			  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_DEVICE_TYPE_RC);
+	val |= SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE;
+	writel(val, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_CTRL);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_stop_link(struct dw_pcie *pci)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = to_sun60i_a733_pcie(pci);
+	u32 val;
+
+	val = readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_CTRL);
+	val &= ~SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE;
+	writel(val, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_CTRL);
+}
+
+static bool sun60i_a733_pcie_link_up(struct dw_pcie *pci)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = to_sun60i_a733_pcie(pci);
+	u32 val = readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_STAT);
+
+	/*
+	 * Both halves, not either: SMLH is the physical layer and RDLH the data
+	 * link layer, and a link that reports SMLH alone has trained but cannot
+	 * carry a TLP yet.
+	 */
+	return (val & (SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SMLH_LINK_UP |
+		       SUN60I_A733_PCIE_RDLH_LINK_UP)) ==
+	       (SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SMLH_LINK_UP | SUN60I_A733_PCIE_RDLH_LINK_UP);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Empty, and required: dw_pcie_host_init() dereferences pp->ops without
+ * checking it (pcie-designware-host.c, `if (pp->ops->init)`), so a NULL here is
+ * an oops during probe rather than a default. Nothing in this controller needs
+ * a host callback -- the core's own init path does everything.
+ */
+static const struct dw_pcie_host_ops sun60i_a733_pcie_host_ops = {
+};
+
+static const struct dw_pcie_ops sun60i_a733_dw_pcie_ops = {
+	.start_link	= sun60i_a733_pcie_start_link,
+	.stop_link	= sun60i_a733_pcie_stop_link,
+	.link_up	= sun60i_a733_pcie_link_up,
+};
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_power_off(void *data)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = data;
+
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(pcie->perst, 1);
+	phy_power_off(pcie->phy);
+	phy_exit(pcie->phy);
+	reset_control_assert(pcie->rst_its);
+	reset_control_assert(pcie->rst_core);
+	reset_control_assert(pcie->rst_pwrup);
+	clk_bulk_disable_unprepare(ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks), pcie->clks);
+	if (pcie->vpcie3v3)
+		regulator_disable(pcie->vpcie3v3);
+}
+
+static int sun60i_a733_pcie_power_on(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	struct device *dev = pcie->pci.dev;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (pcie->vpcie3v3) {
+		ret = regulator_enable(pcie->vpcie3v3);
+		if (ret)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "vpcie3v3\n");
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The AXI slave clock has to be 400 MHz. Its mux resets to PERI0_600M,
+	 * and the controller does not survive that: configuration reads into
+	 * the slave window never complete and the CPU that issued one hangs
+	 * with interrupts off -- measured on 2026-09-21, a hard lockup during
+	 * the first scan of bus 01, with the link trained and every gate open.
+	 *
+	 * The vendor BSP sets the same rate from its own clock setup and marks
+	 * this controller generation with a `pcie_slv_clk_400m` flag; the UM
+	 * lists 010 = PERI0_400M as a source of this mux without saying it is
+	 * required. So the number is the vendor's and the failure it prevents
+	 * is ours, measured here.
+	 */
+	ret = clk_set_rate(pcie->clks[SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AXI_SLV].clk,
+			   400000000);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "axi-slv clock to 400 MHz\n");
+
+	ret = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks), pcie->clks);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_regulator;
+
+	/*
+	 * Power-up reset first, then the controller, then the ITS in front of
+	 * it. The order is the one the UM's own bring-up sequence uses, and it
+	 * is the only one in which the controller sees a bus that is already
+	 * clocked when it leaves reset.
+	 */
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(pcie->rst_pwrup);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_clocks;
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(pcie->rst_core);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_pwrup;
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(pcie->rst_its);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_core;
+
+	ret = phy_init(pcie->phy);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_its;
+	ret = phy_power_on(pcie->phy);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_phy_init;
+
+	/*
+	 * PERST# comes off last, once the reference clock the PHY generates is
+	 * live, and the device gets its 100 ms before anything asks it a
+	 * question. Skipping this wait is how a drive that is perfectly healthy
+	 * enumerates on some boots and not others.
+	 */
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(pcie->perst, 0);
+	msleep(SUN60I_A733_PCIE_T_PVPERL_MS);
+
+	return 0;
+
+err_phy_init:
+	phy_exit(pcie->phy);
+err_its:
+	reset_control_assert(pcie->rst_its);
+err_core:
+	reset_control_assert(pcie->rst_core);
+err_pwrup:
+	reset_control_assert(pcie->rst_pwrup);
+err_clocks:
+	clk_bulk_disable_unprepare(ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks), pcie->clks);
+err_regulator:
+	if (pcie->vpcie3v3)
+		regulator_disable(pcie->vpcie3v3);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static int sun60i_a733_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie;
+	int ret, i;
+
+	pcie = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*pcie), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!pcie)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	pcie->pci.dev = dev;
+	pcie->pci.ops = &sun60i_a733_dw_pcie_ops;
+	pcie->pci.pp.ops = &sun60i_a733_pcie_host_ops;
+
+	pcie->app = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "app");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->app))
+		return PTR_ERR(pcie->app);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks); i++)
+		pcie->clks[i].id = sun60i_a733_pcie_clks[i];
+	ret = devm_clk_bulk_get(dev, ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks), pcie->clks);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "clocks\n");
+
+	pcie->rst_pwrup = devm_reset_control_get_exclusive(dev, "pwrup");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->rst_pwrup))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->rst_pwrup), "pwrup reset\n");
+	pcie->rst_core = devm_reset_control_get_exclusive(dev, "core");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->rst_core))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->rst_core), "core reset\n");
+	pcie->rst_its = devm_reset_control_get_exclusive(dev, "its");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->rst_its))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->rst_its), "its reset\n");
+
+	pcie->phy = devm_phy_get(dev, "pcie-phy");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->phy))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->phy), "phy\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * PERST# is a board signal, and on the A7S it is the only way the
+	 * device is reset: the controller's internal PERST generator
+	 * (SUBSYS_PCIE_BGR bit1) is not wired to the connector.
+	 */
+	pcie->perst = devm_gpiod_get(dev, "reset", GPIOD_OUT_HIGH);
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->perst))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->perst), "PERST gpio\n");
+
+	pcie->vpcie3v3 = devm_regulator_get_optional(dev, "vpcie3v3");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->vpcie3v3)) {
+		if (PTR_ERR(pcie->vpcie3v3) != -ENODEV)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->vpcie3v3),
+					     "vpcie3v3\n");
+		pcie->vpcie3v3 = NULL;
+	}
+
+	ret = sun60i_a733_pcie_power_on(pcie);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, sun60i_a733_pcie_power_off, pcie);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, pcie);
+
+	ret = dw_pcie_host_init(&pcie->pci.pp);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "host init\n");
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
+
+	dw_pcie_host_deinit(&pcie->pci.pp);
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id sun60i_a733_pcie_of_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-pcie" },
+	{ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, sun60i_a733_pcie_of_match);
+
+static struct platform_driver sun60i_a733_pcie_driver = {
+	.probe	= sun60i_a733_pcie_probe,
+	.remove	= sun60i_a733_pcie_remove,
+	.driver	= {
+		.name		= "sun60i-a733-pcie",
+		.of_match_table	= sun60i_a733_pcie_of_match,
+		.suppress_bind_attrs = true,
+	},
+};
+module_platform_driver(sun60i_a733_pcie_driver);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Allwinner A733 PCIe root complex");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
